28	\begin{abstract}
29	%==============================================================================
30	Probe-based calibration methods can detect whether a language model's internal representations encode answer correctness, but all existing approaches are English-only. We present the first large-scale study of cross-lingual correctness probe transfer, spanning three architectures (Qwen3-235B, Llama~4 Scout, Gemma~4 31B Dense), 50 languages, and five transfer conditions. Our analysis reveals four interconnected findings forming a causal chain. \textbf{(1)}~A depth-transferability Pareto frontier whose strength is architecture-dependent: Qwen3 exhibits a strong, consistent tradeoff (per-language Spearman $\rho$ significant in 15/20 languages) while Llama~4 shows a weak, language-specific pattern (6/20 significant; three languages exhibit reversed trends). \textbf{(2)}~Decomposing correctness into difficulty-shared (65\% variance, 12\% of transfer degradation) and capability-specific (35\% variance, 88\% of degradation) components reveals that capability-specific representations drive deep-layer transfer failure. \textbf{(3)}~CaCLR, a consistency-regularized calibration method requiring no target-language labels, provides a mean improvement of +3.5\pp{} AUROC over zero-shot transfer, with gains concentrated at deep layers (+4.8\pp). \textbf{(4)}~A Simpson's Paradox in the CKA--transfer relationship---raw correlation is negative ($\rho = -0.36$ to $-0.41$; depth confound) but partial correlation controlling for depth is significantly positive ($\rho = +0.41$ to $+0.48$; $p < 0.01$ on both architectures)---reveals that within-layer cross-lingual CKA genuinely predicts transfer quality while the correctness direction occupies a near-universal subspace (within-layer angle $12.4^{\circ}$). We additionally find that 28\% of low-resource probing results are capacity artifacts, and that Procrustes alignment reverses direction at a threshold of $\sim$0.52 zero-shot AUROC. Code and probes will be released.
31	\end{abstract}
