34	\section{Introduction}
35	\label{sec:intro}
36	%==============================================================================
37	
38	Multilingual large language models suffer from a persistent calibration gap: non-English languages exhibit expected calibration error (ECE) up to five times worse than English \cite{joshi2024llm}. Output-based recalibration methods such as temperature scaling require per-language calibration data, while ensemble approaches like MKA incur multiplicative inference costs \cite{zhou2024lace}. Recent work on probe-based calibration offers an alternative: linear probes trained on internal representations can detect whether a model ``knows'' the answer is correct, and this correctness direction is nearly orthogonal to the model's verbalized confidence \cite{miao2026coral,miao2026steering}. However, the entire probe-based calibration literature is English-only.
39	
40	Can English-trained correctness probes transfer to other languages without target-language labels? If so, under what conditions, and why does transfer succeed or fail? These questions are fundamental to the viability of representation-level calibration beyond English. Xiao et al.\ \cite{xiao2025crosslingual} transferred \emph{knowledge boundary} probes---binary answerability classifiers at the input level---and found encouraging results in 8 languages. But correctness probing targets a fundamentally different signal: output-dependent accuracy on specific items, not input-level answerability. The cross-lingual properties of correctness representations remain unexplored.
41	
42	We present the first large-scale study of cross-lingual correctness probe transfer. Probing three architectures (Qwen3-235B-A22B, Llama~4 Scout, Gemma~4 31B Dense) across 20--50 typologically diverse languages on GlobalMMLU, we discover a \emph{depth-transferability Pareto frontier}: deeper layers yield better English probes but progressively degrade in zero-shot cross-lingual transfer. The frontier's strength is architecture-dependent---strong and consistent on Qwen3, weak and language-specific on Llama~4. A geometric analysis based on centered kernel alignment (CKA) reveals a Simpson's Paradox at the heart of this phenomenon: pooling across layers, CKA and transfer quality correlate \emph{negatively} (depth confound), but controlling for layer depth, higher within-layer CKA significantly predicts better transfer ($p < 0.01$ on both architectures). The correctness direction itself occupies a near-universal low-dimensional subspace (cross-language angle $12.4^{\circ}$ within each layer) that persists even as global representations specialize by language.
43	
44	Our contributions are:
45	\begin{enumerate}
46	\item \textbf{Pareto discovery.} We identify an architecture-dependent depth-transferability tradeoff across three architectures and 20 languages, with Pareto knee at 55--58\% depth.
47	\item \textbf{Decomposition analysis.} We decompose correctness into difficulty-shared and capability-specific components, showing that capability drives 88\% of transfer degradation despite accounting for only 35\% of variance.
48	\item \textbf{CaCLR method.} A consistency-regularized calibration approach providing +3.5\pp{} mean AUROC gain without target-language labels, with gains concentrated at deep layers.
49	\item \textbf{CKA geometric explanation.} The Simpson's Paradox, subspace persistence ($12.4^{\circ}$ within-layer, $38.7^{\circ}$ cross-layer), and CKA cliff threshold ($\sim$0.35) together explain when and why transfer succeeds.
